### A KNOWN DEFECT, and the one hole in this suite's tenancy story. Read this before "fixing" it.
###
### `work_orders.reference` is declared `unique`. The driver emits that as a plain
### `HasIndex(field.Name).IsUnique()` — see `DescriptorModelBuilder.ConfigureField` — with **no
### `tenant_id` in the index**, on a `tenancy: "scoped"` entity. So the constraint is unique across
### the whole instance rather than within a tenant, and tenant B's create collides with a value only
### tenant A holds.
###
### That makes it a **cross-tenant existence oracle**: tenant B learns whether tenant A holds a given
### `reference`, one request per candidate. Everything else in 001 is careful to answer 404 precisely
### so B cannot make that inference; this one channel gives it away.
###
### **The root cause is the index, not the status code.** The neighbouring defect —
### `030-Problems/002`, a duplicate answered 500 rather than 409 — is a *different* problem that
### happens to share a trigger, and mapping the violation to a clean 409 does NOT close this one:
### 409-versus-201 is exactly the same signal to tenant B as 500-versus-201. The fix is a
### tenant-scoped unique index (`HasIndex("tenant_id", field.Name)` on a scoped entity), after which
### BOTH requests below are 201 and this case goes red so it can be rewritten as the
### indistinguishability assertion it wants to be.
###
### The test below therefore asserts DISTINGUISHABILITY, not a status. A status-only change leaves it
### green, which is correct — the leak would still be there.

### Tenant south creates a work order whose `reference` only TENANT NORTH holds.
# @name ProbeAValueAnotherTenantHolds
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [500]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantSouth}}",
  "reference": "WO-1001",
  "title": "Oracle probe",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "access_code": "ORACLE",
  "customer_id": "{{SouthCustomerId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The identical request with a `reference` NO tenant holds. The only difference between the two is
### whether the other tenant happens to hold the value — which is precisely the fact B must not learn.
# @name ProbeAValueNoTenantHolds
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantSouth}}",
  "reference": "WO-9911",
  "title": "Oracle probe",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "access_code": "ORACLE",
  "customer_id": "{{SouthCustomerId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The refused probe wrote nothing into tenant south
# @name SouthAfterTheProbe
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

### ...and tenant north's row is untouched by either probe
# @name NorthsRowAfterTheProbe
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### Clean up the row the second probe created, so later groups measure the set they expect
# @name RemoveTheProbeRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [204]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{ProbeAValueNoTenantHolds.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
